# POD Combat — System Reference

Single source of truth for the combat minigame's runtime model. Code
files reference sections of this doc instead of re-stating the
contract in their own headers.

If you're reading this for the first time, scan the **Architecture
overview** below, then jump to whatever section the file you opened
points at.

---

## Architecture overview

Group combat uses a **10-slot system** (slots 0-4 = player team, 5-9
= enemy team). Per-combatant state lives on `POD_combat_slot`
story_cycle scopes: one story per slot, created at combat init and
cleaned up at combat end. Pointer variables `pod_combat_slot_0..9`
on the combat host (the PC) hand back the matching story.

A shared **workspace** (`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`)
holds whichever combatant is acting and whichever one is the current
target; the workspace is loaded from / saved back to the appropriate
slot story at each action boundary by
`pod_combat_{load,save}_workspace_effect`. Skill effects read and
write only the workspace, so the same code runs for the
PC, AI coterie, the primary opponent, and knights.

Combat hits four supporting story_cycle scopes besides slots:
`POD_combat_move` (the move catalogue), `POD_combat_log_entry` (rows
in the rolling log), `POD_combat_persistent` (encounter-level state
held on the host), and the per-actor "AI move history" pointers
saved on the acting AI character.

**Hit chance formula:** `60 + (own prowess − target prowess) / 2`,
clamped `20-95`.

