## §4 Result codes

Single-int outcome model held in `pod_combat_last_result_code` on
the host. Replaces an earlier 11-flag `pod_combat_last_was_*` scheme
(see **Internal milestone glossary** § B1). Helpers along the damage
and heal pipelines write directly to this var; `push_log` reads it
unconditionally and dispatches to the matching log loc key.

| Code | Outcome |
|---:|---|
| -1 | sentinel — "no code yet" |
| 0 | HP damage |
| 1 | guard-blocked |
| 2 | soaked |
| 3 | missed |
| 4 | reserved (was "countered"; never written by current code, kept reserved so the existing rcode numbering doesn't shift if a future move type needs the slot) |
| 5 | dodged |
| 6 | HP damage + retaliation triggered |
| 7 | HP damage + retaliation soaked |
| 8 | utility move applied (no damage; default if no helper writes a code) |
| 9 | morale damage only |
| 10 | HP healed |
| 11 | morale healed |
| 12 | stealth-triggered auto-miss (distinct from rcode 5 dodged so the loc line links to the Stealth concept, not Dodge) |

**Sentinel handling:** Callers default `pod_combat_last_result_code`
to -1 in §2 of `POD_execute_combat_move`, then helpers write their
code as the move resolves. `push_log` defaults to 8 when the final
value is still -1 (utility / no-op moves).

**Priority semantics:** Most damage-side outcomes are SET by a single
mutually-exclusive branch in the damage pipeline, so a plain
`set_variable` is sufficient. The exceptions:

- Retaliation OVERLAYS damage: `0→6`, `2→7` conditionally
  (`pod_combat_set_result_code_promote_retaliation`).
- Secondary effects (morale damage, heal) only set their code if no
  primary HP-damage code has fired
  (`pod_combat_set_result_code_if_unset`).

**Self-damage snapshot/restore.** §8 stashes
`pod_combat_last_damage` + `pod_combat_last_result_code` into
`pod_combat_pre_self_last_damage` + `pod_combat_pre_self_result_code`,
fires the bypass-guard damage helper (which overwrites both with the
self-hit's outcome), then restores from the snapshot so the log line
reflects the MAIN hit's number and rcode. The B1 result-code
consolidation cut the snapshot from three fields to two; the
restore-then-call pattern itself is still load-bearing.

**Result-code partition triggers** (in
`pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt`) compose against the rcode
to express the rider/reaction gates:

- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger` — codes
  0/1/2/6/7 (HP damage / blocked / soaked / retaliation overlays).
- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_miss_trigger` — codes 3/5/12
  (miss / dodge / stealth).
- `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger` — codes
  0/6/7 (a wound went through).

### Log dispatcher

`PODCombatLogResultText` customizable_localization
(`pod_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`) consumes three saved scopes:

- `entry` — the log entry story scope, for `var:damage` / `heal_amount`
  / `morale_damage_amount` / `morale_heal_amount` reads
- `move` — the executing move story, for `damage_type` / `damage_source`
  / `targeting` / buff-tier checks the AoE intercept and rider
  customizable_locs need
- `rcode` — a value-scope wrapping `result_code`, for the dispatch
  triggers

Each trigger branch checks `scope:rcode` against an integer; matching
branch resolves to the corresponding loc key. `rcodes 0/6/7/10`
split into `_aggravated` variants in the dispatch — the wound-box
icon swaps from neutral (superficial) to red (aggravated) based on
`scope:move.var:damage_type` / `heal_type`.

